comments.body:
Great work @participant1! The AST-based pre-validation approach is exactly what this issue needed -- blocking dangerous imports and calls at the syntax level before execution is the right defence-in-depth layer.

The configurable blocklist + block/warn modes give teams flexibility to adopt incrementally without breaking existing workflows.

@langchain-ai/core-maintainers -- would be great to get participant1 assigned so PR #35828 can be reopened. Happy to review the security aspects if useful.

@participant2 thanks -- that middleware pattern sounds like a clean fit.

Here is a rough sketch of how MCPS passport verification could slot into the AgentShield callback chain:

```js
// AgentShield callback middleware (before risk scoring)
async function verifyIdentity(event) {
  const passport = event.metadata?.mcps_passport;
  if (!passport) return { verified: false, agent_id: null };

  const { valid, passport: parsed } = mcps.verifyPassport(passport);
  if (!valid) return { verified: false, agent_id: null };

  // Anchor all downstream risk scores to verified identity
  event.verified_agent_id = parsed.agent_id;
  event.trust_level = parsed.trust_level;
  return { verified: true, agent_id: parsed.agent_id };
}
```

Key points:
- Runs before risk analysis -- every score is anchored to a cryptographically verified agent_id
- No self-reported sessions -- identity comes from the signed passport (ECDSA P-256)
- Trust level from the passport can inform risk thresholds (L0-L4 maps to different scoring baselines)
- Stateless -- AgentShield just calls `verifyPassport()`, no need to manage keys

The npm package is `mcp-secure` (v1.0.2) if you want to test against it. Happy to iterate on the interface shape if the callback pipeline needs it structured differently.

@participant3 regex-based input scanning is a useful first-pass filter for known patterns at the content layer.

Worth noting this operates at a different level than what the thread is building towards. MCPS handles cryptographic identity and message integrity at the protocol level -- verifying _who_ sent a message, whether it was tampered with in transit, and whether the calling agent is trusted. Pattern matching catches known-bad payloads in content.

Defence in depth means you want both: identity verification at the transport layer (MCPS) and content inspection at the application layer (scanners). They are complementary.

@reporter Thank you for the invitation — I'm honored to be included alongside the MCPS project's foundation submissions.

I'd be happy to accept contributor access. AgentShield already implements MCPS trust levels and alert thresholds based on L0-L4, so contributing back to the spec aligns naturally with what we're building.

Looking forward to collaborating on the repos and the IETF draft in the future.
